ng before opening the center.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nCastling kingside immediately is a high‑priority objective in open positions when there’s no immediate tactical blow. It brings king to safety, connects rooks, and prepares for central pawn pushes later without worrying about back‑rank issues.  \nQf3-c6 looks tempting but it’s premature — Black could respond with …Nb8‑d7 or …Qd8‑c7 and gain time attacking the queen.  \nB: b5-a6 is less effective because Black can just play …Bb7 later, trading it off comfortably.  \n\nThe best practical move here is to castle to consolidate.  \n\n**Answer:** K: e1-g1'}
